WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.862
Three places in the shared
runbook cause most of the outages.

2
00:00:03.862 --> 00:00:05.137
Precedence first.

3
00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:10.723
Moving the existing fleet into Intune
usually goes through Group Policy triggered

4
00:00:10.723 --> 00:00:15.988
automatic enrollment of hybrid joined
devices: no reimage, no user interaction.

5
00:00:15.988 --> 00:00:21.313
Confirm with dsregcmd that the device shows
both Azure AD joined and domain joined.

6
00:00:21.313 --> 00:00:25.212
But while the GPOs are still
linked, overlaps are dangerous.

7
00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:29.637
When the same policy is set in
both, Group Policy takes precedence.

8
00:00:29.637 --> 00:00:34.249
MDM wins over GP reverses
that only for the Policy CSP.

9
00:00:34.249 --> 00:00:37.725
For the Defender CSP
and others, it's a race.

10
00:00:37.725 --> 00:00:41.279
And Windows Hello for Business,
in the PassportForWork CSP,

11
00:00:41.279 --> 00:00:45.775
won't take the Intune settings until
the Group Policy settings are cleared.

12
00:00:45.775 --> 00:00:49.975
So document every overlap before
Intune enforces anything.

13
00:00:49.975 --> 00:00:51.937
NTLM second.

14
00:00:51.937 --> 00:00:54.908
It's deprecated in all versions,

15
00:00:54.908 --> 00:01:00.163
and NTLMv1 is removed in
Windows 11 24H2 and Server 2025.

16
00:01:00.163 --> 00:01:05.425
A common mistake is treating LAN
Manager level 5 as block all NTLM.

17
00:01:05.425 --> 00:01:12.149
Level 5 makes domain controllers refuse LM
and NTLMv1, but they still accept NTLMv2.

18
00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:15.950
Blocking is done by the
Restrict NTLM policies.

19
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.976
So stage it: audit, refuse the
legacy variants, then deny all,

20
00:01:19.976 --> 00:01:23.415
removing exceptions as
each dependency retires,

21
00:01:23.415 --> 00:01:28.700
and only after a fourteen to thirty day
audit and application owner sign-off.

22
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.100
Conditional Access can't help here:

23
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.900
a Kerberos or NTLM exchange with a
domain controller never reaches it.

24
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.150
SPNs third.

25
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:41.918
A missing SPN is the silent
case: Kerberos isn't attempted,

26
00:01:41.918 --> 00:01:46.787
and NTLM usually succeeds, until
stage 3 makes it an outage.

27
00:01:46.787 --> 00:01:48.837
A duplicate is the loud case.

28
00:01:48.837 --> 00:01:51.868
Validate each repair by
purging the ticket cache

29
00:01:51.868 --> 00:01:54.820
and checking that SQL
Server reports Kerberos.
